    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if the lisinopril is basically stopping a conversion step from happening, how would u describe that interaction visually? like how does the drug actually 'interfere' with the enzyme to stop it from doing its job?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Visually, you can think of the ACE inhibitor like a key that doesn't fit the lock of the ACE enzyme, effectively blocking its active site. The drug molecule temporarily binds to the enzyme, preventing the substrate (Angiotensin I) from attaching and being converted. This inhibition is often competitive or non-competitive, meaning the drug occupies the necessary space, thus halting the chemical reaction that produces the powerful Angiotensin II.",
      "source": "target",
